{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claimed equivalence for obstruction structures is false. A stable mixed configuration can have bichromatic edges forming a matching and every monochromatic component of minimum degree at least 2, yet admit no perfect matching M' extending M whose complement is a 2-factor. Example: take K4 on vertices {c,a1,a2,a3} plus a triangle {b1,b2,b3}, with matching edges a_i-b_i. Color c and all a_i A, and all b_i B. The bichromatic edges are exactly the matching a_i-b_i; every vertex has at least two same-color neighbors, so the configuration is stable. But M already covers a1,a2,a3, so the unmatched vertex c has all neighbors already matched, and no extension to a perfect matching exists. Thus the target's characterization of locally stable mixed configurations is incorrect, and the subsequent count of obstructions via perfect matchings does not cover the actual obstruction class.","counterexample":["3-regular graph: K4 on {c,a1,a2,a3}, triangle on {b1,b2,b3}, plus matching a_i-b_i; color c,a_i=A and b_i=B. Stable but no perfect matching extension."],"target":"4c65c1e95b4d5860ece5aa852ab7743b38d534d06aa95c98220c42ba480f4cd2"}]}